# Generated artefacts are compared byte-for-byte against a fresh generation by
# their `:check` scripts. Without pinning, `core.autocrlf` rewrites them to CRLF
# on Windows checkouts while the generator writes LF, and the check fails on a
# clean tree — a gate that cries wolf is a gate people learn to skip.
#
# The site content tree is written by `apps/site/generate` with LF on every run,
# so on Windows these would otherwise show as modified with an EMPTY diff after
# any generation. That permanent phantom churn is worse than cosmetic: it invites
# a blanket `git checkout -- apps/site` that silently reverts real edits.
apps/site/content/** text eol=lf

# Rust source and manifests are generated and compared byte-for-byte in places
# (`crates/aex-telemetry-schema/src/generated.rs`), and `core.autocrlf=true` on
# Windows would rewrite them to CRLF while the generator writes LF.
*.rs text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
Cargo.lock text eol=lf

# The contract bundle, the published schemas, the two plane documents and the
# conformance corpus are compared byte-for-byte by
# `cargo run -p aex-contract-gen -- check`. Same rule, same reason.
api/generated/** text eol=lf
api/openapi/** text eol=lf
api/schemas/** text eol=lf
conformance/** text eol=lf

# The derived test registry and the unearned-evidence ledger are rewritten by
# `aex-workspace-check registry build` on every manifest change and are meant to
# be regenerated on conflict, like `Cargo.lock`. Without this, the tool writes LF,
# Windows checks out CRLF, and every regeneration reports a whole-file diff.
release/*.json text eol=lf
release/policy/** text eol=lf

# The regional table generation definitions and the bundle Terraform consumes
# are rebuilt byte-for-byte by `aex-regional-test-support`'s bundler and asserted
# against the checked-in file. Same rule, same reason.
migrations/regional/** text eol=lf
migrations/central/** text eol=lf
